# Who must review a change before it is merged.
#
# The rules are read from top to bottom and the last matching one wins, so
# the general rule stands first and the security-sensitive paths after it.
# The owner reviews everything today; the explicit paths below say which
# files never pass on a glance even when the rest of a pull request is
# routine, and they stay named when more reviewers are added.
#
# The file works as a gate only when the branch protection of main requires
# a review from a code owner; GitHub does not enforce it by itself.

*       @ultherego

# The decision whether a principal may do a thing at all.
/internal/authz/        @ultherego

# What the root helper is allowed to be asked for, and by whom.
/internal/helpercap/    @ultherego
/internal/helper/       @ultherego

# The keys of the installation and the state that says which of them is
# current: a mistake here loses the fleet's identity rather than a feature.
/internal/cryptostate/  @ultherego

# The fleet certificate authority and everything it signs.
/internal/pki/          @ultherego

# The door the agents knock on: authentication of a host, the session, the
# envelope that carries every task.
/internal/gateway/      @ultherego

# The schema is applied on a customer's database and is not taken back.
/db/migrations/         @ultherego

# The workflows hold the gates themselves; a change here can switch a check
# off without anything else in the tree changing.
/.github/               @ultherego
